WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.929 --> 00:00:13.419
In the next few decades, the world as we know
it will change, a lot.

00:00:13.419 --> 00:00:16.910
This will of course bring many opportunities...
but also many challenges.

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:21.370
More precisely, most developed countries are
facing such a large threat that their response

00:00:21.370 --> 00:00:26.320
may determine, in economic, political and
social terms, which countries succeed and

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:27.320
which fail.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:33.340
Folks, I'm talking about... population aging,
of the demographic pyramid’s 180-degree

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:35.570
turn.

00:00:35.570 --> 00:00:43.489
Yes, I’m sure this isn’t news, that most
of you have already heard about this subject…

00:00:43.489 --> 00:00:44.489
however...

00:00:44.489 --> 00:00:47.620
Have you stopped to think about this process’s
consequences?

00:00:47.620 --> 00:00:50.100
Are your governments doing something about
it?

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:54.829
Have they come up with something like a contingency
plan to face the risks that lie ahead?

00:00:54.829 --> 00:00:59.090
Listen up.

00:00:59.090 --> 00:01:06.509
(THE WORLD FACING A NEW STAGE)

00:01:06.509 --> 00:01:10.079
In about 100 years, our world will have changed
a lot.

00:01:10.079 --> 00:01:13.360
It has changed so much that if we could get
a time machine, say for example, Doc's Delorean,

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:20.240
and travel 2 or 3 centuries back in time for
a season… sooner rather than later most

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:23.400
of us, if not all, would want to rush back
home.

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:27.869
We’d be missing loads of things; we’d
be horrified by the day to day; we’d be

00:01:27.869 --> 00:01:35.460
forced to work intensely to survive; we’d
be afraid of getting sick, even of not being

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:36.460
able to eat, etc., etc.

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:39.370
But... let's stay on the subject…

00:01:39.370 --> 00:01:44.880
The fact is that at the end of the 19th century;
the world’s population surpassed the 1.5

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:46.360
billion person barrier.

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:53.270
And yet… only 100 years later, during the
20th century, the world population had multiplied

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:59.000
by 4 and surpassed 6 billion people.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:05.700
And of course, considering the enormous speed
with which the world population grew, we can’t

00:02:05.700 --> 00:02:10.110
be surprised by the fact that many experts
began to worry about overpopulation.

00:02:10.110 --> 00:02:15.659
And don’t think this was just a thing among
mediocre experts, scientists and politicians.

00:02:15.659 --> 00:02:22.579
In 1974, a report by the Secretary of State,
Henry Kissinger, urged Richard Nixon’s government

00:02:22.579 --> 00:02:27.230
to consider birth control in countries such
as Brazil, India, Mexico, Colombia, Ethiopia

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:31.629
and Thailand as a top priority.

00:02:31.629 --> 00:02:35.230
According to the report, the “demographic
explosion” that was taking place posed a

00:02:35.230 --> 00:02:39.890
serious threat to the very security of the
United States.

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:43.370
Sure, with so many people consuming resources...

00:02:43.370 --> 00:02:47.930
Kissinger thought we were heading towards
a world of scarcity and fierce struggles for

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:48.930
resources.

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:52.620
Have you noticed how Malthusian arguments
come up again and again even though they never

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:54.529
actually happen?

00:02:54.529 --> 00:02:59.650
The fact is that while guys like Kissinger
tried to think about how to control the world

00:02:59.650 --> 00:03:03.659
population and “influence” their decisions...
a change was already taking place, such a

00:03:03.659 --> 00:03:12.279
huge change that it would turn into an entirely
new point in the debate.

00:03:12.279 --> 00:03:21.169
In the last 50 years, world fertility has
halved, so much so that nowadays Western governments

00:03:21.169 --> 00:03:24.959
no longer think about overpopulation... but
about aging.

00:03:24.959 --> 00:03:29.349
Today European countries and even the United
States tend to lose native inhabitants.

00:03:29.349 --> 00:03:30.620
Check this out:

00:03:30.620 --> 00:03:37.590
In Germany, for example, since 1972 there
have been 5 million more deaths than births.

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:42.430
However, the most extreme case has been that
of Russia, where in just 20 years, between

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:49.599
1991 and 2011, there were 13.13 million more
deaths than births.

00:03:49.599 --> 00:03:53.779
Future projections show that in the next 50
years, Europe’s native population will decrease

00:03:53.779 --> 00:03:57.029
by around 100 million people.

00:03:57.029 --> 00:04:03.599
And not only that... the other effect of this
entire process... is that the world population,

00:04:03.599 --> 00:04:08.339
especially that of the most developed countries
is aging at full speed... and that, my friends,

00:04:08.339 --> 00:04:10.919
is posing many, many challenges.

00:04:10.919 --> 00:04:16.010
Pensions, health care expenses, dependence...
and the question is, how on earth can we finance

00:04:16.010 --> 00:04:17.930
this gigantic expense?

00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:21.040
To give you an idea of what we’re talking
about, it’s estimated that in many European

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:26.930
countries, the percentage of the population
over 65 years will be of around 40% by the

00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:27.930
year 2050.

00:04:27.930 --> 00:04:30.390
And one of those most affected countries is,
precisely...

00:04:30.390 --> 00:04:31.390
Spain.

00:04:31.390 --> 00:04:34.810
Listen up.

00:04:34.810 --> 00:04:40.259
(A COUNTRY OF RETIRED PEOPLE?)

00:04:40.259 --> 00:04:42.599
Here’s an exercise…

00:04:42.599 --> 00:04:47.300
Let’s assume that fertility in Spain and
in the European Union remained within the

00:04:47.300 --> 00:04:52.070
rates that they’ve had since the year 2000
and that, in addition, no immigrants arrive.

00:04:52.070 --> 00:04:53.759
Do you want to know what would happen?

00:04:53.759 --> 00:04:57.449
Well, check out the following graph.

00:04:57.449 --> 00:05:01.870
As you can see, the population aged between
18 and 40 years, which is the population that

00:05:01.870 --> 00:05:06.090
works the most and the population that has
children, would take a huge dip.

00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:11.430
Of course… the number of inhabitants just
a decade later, would follow the same trend.

00:05:11.430 --> 00:05:17.210
Folks, Spain is one of the countries that’s
beginning to suffer the most from this demographic

00:05:17.210 --> 00:05:18.210
crisis.

00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:23.490
( "The demographic crisis is getting worse:
Spain suffers its biggest population loss

00:05:23.490 --> 00:05:25.360
in 2017".)

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:29.110
If we take a look at the average number of
children per woman, we can truly understand

00:05:29.110 --> 00:05:40.060
what’s going on.

00:05:40.060 --> 00:05:44.629
And this is taking into account that the arrival
of immigrants in the last 20 years has boosted

00:05:44.629 --> 00:05:45.849
birth rates.

00:05:45.849 --> 00:05:52.560
Nowadays 23% of all children born in Spain,
almost 1 in 4, have a foreign mother.

00:05:52.560 --> 00:06:00.090
But perhaps... the most important data to
predict what’s going to happen will come

00:06:00.090 --> 00:06:03.400
from looking at how the number of people of
working age will evolve.

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:08.150
They, after all, are the ones who generate
the vast majority of resources, both public

00:06:08.150 --> 00:06:09.150
and private.

00:06:09.150 --> 00:06:13.979
Well, if we assume that there’s no immigration...
in just 30 years the working-age Spanish population

00:06:13.979 --> 00:06:18.200
would decrease by 10 million people.... and
according to the projections of the Institute

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:20.340
of Statistics of the Government of Spain...

00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:24.560
Spaniards will face this panorama by 2050:

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:30.180
19 million people of working age – of whom
of course not all would work – and almost

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:32.599
22 million children and retirees...

00:06:32.599 --> 00:06:38.970
That is, the dependent population would be
greater than the working age population.

00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:46.360
We’re talking about Spain, but this scenario
is very similar for most developed countries.

00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:52.389
And, as you can imagine, all this has many
consequences.

00:06:52.389 --> 00:07:00.020
(THE CONSEQUENCES)

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:04.811
In recent times, there’s been a lot of talk
about the risk of robotization... but perhaps

00:07:04.811 --> 00:07:10.770
the most imminent risk that developed economies
have to face is their quick aging process.

00:07:10.770 --> 00:07:16.879
At the end of the day, it’s clear that an
aging population has several consequences.

00:07:16.879 --> 00:07:21.349
For one, it’s foreseeable that if nothing
changes, public spending will skyrocket to

00:07:21.349 --> 00:07:27.750
pay pensions, health care expenses, assistance
and care for the elderly, etc., etc.

00:07:27.750 --> 00:07:32.860
("Pension spending increases by 50% in Spain
in ten years".)

00:07:32.860 --> 00:07:37.279
And no doubt… this will be very clear to
politicians: the hegemony of retired voters

00:07:37.279 --> 00:07:38.279
will be overwhelming.

00:07:38.279 --> 00:07:41.240
It’s a matter of votes.

00:07:41.240 --> 00:07:47.259
But... at the same time, as we’ve seen,
there are going to be far fewer working-age

00:07:47.259 --> 00:07:51.810
people... so the question is: How on earth
are we going to pay for this huge expense?

00:07:51.810 --> 00:07:55.960
Will the already high taxes be raised even
more, further damaging the competitiveness

00:07:55.960 --> 00:08:00.169
of countries with as many economic problems
as Spain or Italy?

00:08:00.169 --> 00:08:02.300
Well... that recipe never works.

00:08:02.300 --> 00:08:11.219
Now, at this point I think we need to answer
a mandatory question: what the hell can we

00:08:11.219 --> 00:08:12.249
do?

00:08:12.249 --> 00:08:16.349
(WE NEED A PLAN)

00:08:16.349 --> 00:08:21.719
I know, politicians don’t usually like to
think about the future, especially future

00:08:21.719 --> 00:08:22.719
problems

00:08:22.719 --> 00:08:23.939
What?

00:08:23.939 --> 00:08:29.520
I need to think about problems that will happen
20 years from now and risk losing votes?

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:31.110
No no no.

00:08:31.110 --> 00:08:32.300
Let someone else take care of that.

00:08:32.300 --> 00:08:36.110
Well, if we don’t want to see headlines
like this, developed countries are going to

00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:37.830
need a plan:

00:08:37.830 --> 00:08:43.789
("Japan's finance minister tells elderly they
should 'hurry up and die' to help reduce country's

00:08:43.789 --> 00:08:45.029
rising welfare bill”.

00:08:45.029 --> 00:08:46.029
Daily Mail.

00:08:46.029 --> 00:08:50.560
The time to make decisions is now, and countries
that decide to look the other way and do nothing

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:54.370
will face many, many problems in the future.

00:08:54.370 --> 00:08:59.640
See, lately, here on VISUALPOLITIK, we've
talked a lot about immigration.

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:05.600
Well, experience tells us that this is a good
way to stop population aging and buy time

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:07.209
to make new reforms.

00:09:07.209 --> 00:09:13.170
(“As Australia, Hong Kong, New Zealand and
Singapore have shown, immigration can soften

00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:14.830
the blow from rapid aging.”

00:09:14.830 --> 00:09:19.680
Changyong Rhee, Director of the Asia-Pacific
Department at the International Monetary)

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:24.470
Yes, despite many politicians’ discourse,
the truth is that developed countries will

00:09:24.470 --> 00:09:27.120
need many people to arrive from abroad.

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:31.510
So, folks, instead of thinking about building
walls and closing borders, it would be much

00:09:31.510 --> 00:09:36.700
more advisable to start a system that manages
to attract people from outside in an orderly

00:09:36.700 --> 00:09:39.630
fashion and facilitates their integration.

00:09:39.630 --> 00:09:44.639
Of course, that’s not all.

00:09:44.639 --> 00:09:48.750
As public pension systems will almost certainly
become much less generous – because they

00:09:48.750 --> 00:09:53.170
won’t have much of a budget -, governments
should also be promoting savings... both from

00:09:53.170 --> 00:09:56.529
the governments themselves and from families.

00:09:56.529 --> 00:10:02.610
( "Is any Spanish political party warning
citizens of the inexorable cut in their pensions?

00:10:02.610 --> 00:10:07.890
No, they’re all silent about our true future,
giving a false impression that one doesn’t

00:10:07.890 --> 00:10:11.330
need to save to guarantee a fairly dignified
retirement."

00:10:11.330 --> 00:10:15.790
Juan Ramón Rallo, Economist and Director
of the Juan de Mariana Institute)

00:10:15.790 --> 00:10:20.160
And along with all this, of course, governments
should promote policies that attract companies,

00:10:20.160 --> 00:10:24.920
that promote entrepreneurship and above all,
that favor productivity, investment in machines

00:10:24.920 --> 00:10:27.600
and technology, and in research and development.

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:33.440
Yes, I think robots will greatly help us with
this problem.

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:39.800
Unfortunately, in many of the affected countries,
including Spain, nothing is being done.

00:10:39.800 --> 00:10:43.210
And, finally, before we finish, I’d like
to throw out an idea.

00:10:43.210 --> 00:10:47.750
See, the effect of aging is accompanied by
a longevity effect.

00:10:47.750 --> 00:10:51.710
Allow me to explain this: thanks to medical
advances, better nutrition and a higher quality

00:10:51.710 --> 00:10:54.940
of life, mortality rates for all ages have
decreased.

00:10:54.940 --> 00:10:58.370
That is, people are younger for longer.

00:10:58.370 --> 00:11:03.930
See, in the United States, for example, a
75-year-old person has the same mortality

00:11:03.930 --> 00:11:07.380
rate today as a 65-year-old in the 1950s.

00:11:07.380 --> 00:11:12.290
In Japan, 80 is the new 65.

00:11:12.290 --> 00:11:16.620
And also, thanks to medical and technological
advances, this evolution continues to grow

00:11:16.620 --> 00:11:17.620
and grow.

00:11:17.620 --> 00:11:18.620
Which is fantastic.

00:11:18.620 --> 00:11:23.040
Therefore... if people now have a longer and
more productive life, wouldn’t it be wise

00:11:23.040 --> 00:11:28.410
to encourage them to continue working for
longer, even partially or in more comfortable

00:11:28.410 --> 00:11:29.410
roles?

00:11:29.410 --> 00:11:30.740
I’ll leave that there.

00:11:30.740 --> 00:11:31.760
But now it's your turn...

00:11:31.760 --> 00:11:35.050
Would you be in favor of increasing your country’s
retirement age?

00:11:35.050 --> 00:11:37.010
Leave your answer in the comments as well
as in the survey.

